      },
      {
         "id": "21bbcdd1-5f72-4a28-abec-44a2c209369c",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "nerd",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "576e4648-77fa-4a6d-9e51-9459ced964f3",
               "text": "Musa, Auwalu (2014)",
               "page": "https://www.academia.edu/9099310/Origin_of_Modern_Mathematical_Numeral_0_1_2_3_4_5_6_7_8_9_the_Hindu_Indian_Brahmagubta_The_Islamo_Arabic_or_the_West",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2014",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "da2e2b37-7b63-4ba8-8ddd-e72535068258",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=da2e2b37-7b63-4ba8-8ddd-e72535068258",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Reading the Rule\n\nThe recipe has only one line, but we can extract important information from it:\n\n**0 = 0 × N**\n\nRead it aloud: \"zero equals zero times N.\"\n\n- On the right side, **0** and **N** are the two **factors** being multiplied.\n- On the left side is the **product** — the answer.\n- **N** is a placeholder for any number you like, so one single rule covers every multiplication by zero at once.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
